            </table>
          </div>
          <br>
          <div style="border: 1px solid #c9c9c9; padding: 8px; margin: 0 0 8px 0;">
            <div style="font-weight: bold; margin: 0 0 4px 0;">Example 2: Diagonal Line Not Through the Origin</div>
            <div style="margin: 0 0 4px 0;">Statement: <span style="color: #1167b1; font-weight: bold;">A diagonal line does not pass through the origin.</span></div>
            <table style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 14pt;">
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td style="border: 1px solid #ddd; padding: 5px; width: 38%; font-weight: bold;">Axis</td>
                  <td style="border: 1px solid #ddd; padding: 5px;">None</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td style="border: 1px solid #ddd; padding: 5px; font-weight: bold;">Quadrants</td>
                  <td style="border: 1px solid #ddd; padding: 5px;">3</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td style="border: 1px solid #ddd; padding: 5px; font-weight: bold;">Why?</td>
                  <td style="border: 1px solid #ddd; padding: 5px;">A diagonal line that misses the origin passes through 3 quadrants.</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <br>
          <div style="border: 1px solid #c9c9c9; padding: 8px; margin: 0;">
            <div style="font-weight: bold; margin: 0 0 4px 0;">Example 3: Horizontal and Vertical Lines</div>
            <table style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 14pt;">
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <th style="border: 1px solid #ddd; padding: 5px; background: #f4f4f4; text-align: left;">Statement</th>
                  <th style="border: 1px solid #ddd; padding: 5px; background: #f4f4f4; text-align: left;">Axis</th>
                  <th style="border: 1px solid #ddd; padding: 5px; background: #f4f4f4; text-align: left;">Quadrants</th>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td style="border: 1px solid #ddd; padding: 5px;">A horizontal line is above the origin.</td>
                  <td style="border: 1px solid #ddd; padding: 5px;">Horizontal</td>
                  <td style="border: 1px solid #ddd; padding: 5px;">2</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td style="border: 1px solid #ddd; padding: 5px;">A vertical line is to the left of the origin.</td>
                  <td style="border: 1px solid #ddd; padding: 5px;">Vertical</td>
                  <td style="border: 1px solid #ddd; padding: 5px;">2</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </td>
        <td style="width: 42%; vertical-align: top; padding: 0 0 0 6px;">
          <div style="border: 1px solid #c9c9c9; padding: 8px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; background: #fffaf0;">
            <div style="font-weight: bold; font-size: 15pt; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">What to Look For</div>
            <div style="margin: 0 0 4px 0;">1. <strong>Diagonal</strong> means the line is slanted.</div>
            <div style="margin: 0 0 4px 0;">2. <strong>Through the origin</strong> means the line passes through (0, 0).</div>
            <div style="margin: 0 0 4px 0;">3. <strong>Horizontal</strong> means flat left to right.</div>
            <div style="margin: 0 0 4px 0;">4. <strong>Vertical</strong> means straight up and down.</div>
            <div style="margin: 0;">5. The words <strong>above</strong>, <strong>below</strong>, <strong>left</strong>, and <strong>right</strong> help you decide which quadrants are touched.</div>
          </div>
          <br>
          <div style="border: 1px solid #c9c9c9; padding: 8px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; background: #f4fbf4;">
            <div style="font-weight: bold; font-size: 15pt; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">Shortcut</div>
            <table style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 14pt;">
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td style="border: 1px solid #ddd; padding: 5px;">Diagonal through origin</td>
                  <td style="border: 1px solid #ddd; padding: 5px;">None, 2</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td style="border: 1px solid #ddd; padding: 5px;">Diagonal not through origin</td>
                  <td style="border: 1px solid #ddd; padding: 5px;">None, 3</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td style="border: 1px solid #ddd; padding: 5px;">Horizontal line</td>
                  <td style="border: 1px solid #ddd; padding: 5px;">Horizontal, 2</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td style="border: 1px solid #ddd; padding: 5px;">Vertical line</td>
                  <td style="border: 1px solid #ddd; padding: 5px;">Vertical, 2</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <br>
          <div style="border: 1px solid #c9c9c9; padding: 8px; margin: 0 0 8px 0;">
            <div style="font-weight: bold; font-size: 15pt; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">Shortcut Example</div>
            <div style="margin: 0 0 4px 0;">Statement: <strong>A flat line is below the origin.</strong></div>
            <div style="margin: 0 0 4px 0;">Flat line means <span style="color: #a85a00; font-weight: bold;">horizontal</span>.</div>
            <div style="margin: 0 0 4px 0;">Below the origin means it passes through Quadrants III and IV.</div>
            <div style="margin: 0;">Answer: <strong>Horizontal, 2</strong></div>
          </div>
          <br>
          <div style="border: 1px solid #c9c9c9; padding: 8px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; background: #fff5f5;">
            <div style="font-weight: bold; font-size: 15pt; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">Common Mistakes</div>
            <div style="margin: 0 0 5px 0;"><strong>Mistake 1:</strong> Counting the origin as a quadrant. The origin is on the axes.</div>
            <div style="margin: 0 0 5px 0;"><strong>Mistake 2:</strong> Thinking a diagonal line through the origin passes through 4 quadrants. It only passes through 2.</div>
            <div style="margin: 0 0 5px 0;"><strong>Mistake 3:</strong> Confusing horizontal and vertical. Horizontal is flat; vertical is up and down.</div>
            <div style="margin: 0 0 5px 0;"><strong>Mistake 4:</strong> Choosing an axis for a diagonal line. In this problem, diagonal lines use None.</div>
            <div style="margin: 0;"><strong>Mistake 5:</strong> Forgetting that a diagonal line missing the origin passes through 3 quadrants.</div>
          </div>
          <br>
          <div style="border: 1px solid #c9c9c9; padding: 8px; margin: 0; background: #f7f7ff;">
            <div style="font-weight: bold; font-size: 15pt; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">Final Check</div>
            <div style="margin: 0 0 4px 0;">Did you identify the line type?</div>
            <div style="margin: 0 0 4px 0;">Did you check whether the line passes through the origin?</div>
            <div style="margin: 0 0 4px 0;">Did you choose None, Horizontal, or Vertical for the axis?</div>
            <div style="margin: 0;">Did you count only quadrants where the line has points inside the quadrant?</div>
          </div>
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
